# Tcache Double-Free Exploit (glibc 2.31–2.35)

This is a writeup of the classic **tcache poisoning** technique. I'll walk through the vulnerability class, the exploitation strategy, and provide a working PoC against a toy vulnerable program. This is the technique used in many CTF challenges and real-world glibc exploits (e.g., CVE-2021-3326 style bugs).

> **Scope note:** This is educational material for authorized testing / CTF / lab environments. Only run against binaries you own or have permission to test.

---

## 1. Background: tcache internals

In glibc ≥ 2.26, `tcache` is a per-thread cache of freed chunks, indexed by size class (up to 1032 bytes). Each bin holds up to 7 chunks in a singly-linked LIFO list. The metadata lives in the **user data area** of the freed chunk:

```c
struct tcache_entry {
    struct tcache_entry *next;   // pointer to next free chunk in bin
    uint16_t key;                // "double free" detection cookie
};
```

The `key` field is set to `tcache` (the per-thread pointer) on free, and cleared on allocation. glibc 2.29+ added a check: on free, if `e->key == tcache`, it walks the bin and aborts on a detected double free. **However**, this check is trivially bypassed by corrupting `key` before the second free — which is exactly what a "double free" bug lets us do.

